WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.509
All right, in this section, we're gonna be talking

2
00:00:03.509 --> 00:00:07.740
about the LED volume, its hardware, and its processing.

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.519
All right, here at the latte stage in Culver City,

4
00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:13.099
the wall that we utilize implements

5
00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:16.300
Black Pearl II panels from Rho,

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.300
and these panels are 176 pixels by 176 pixels,

7
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.780
and they have a pixel pitch of 2.8 millimeters.

8
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.260
We have two walls.

9
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.500
We have our main wall and a side right-angled wall here.

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.700
The main wall is 10 panels across by five panels down,

11
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.700
which give us a resolution of 1,076 pixels by 880 pixels,

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.500
while the right-angled wall is a five-by-five panel wall.

13
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.359
It's 880 pixels by 880 pixels.

14
00:00:49.359 --> 00:00:50.780
We also have a wild wall,

15
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.920
which is an 85-inch 4K Sony television.

16
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.840
The resolution for it is 3,840 by 2,160,

17
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.159
but we have our television rotated vertically,

18
00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:06.659
so it would be 2,160 by 3,840.

19
00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.579
This particular configuration

20
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:11.620
is a great small stage configuration.

21
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.140
It works well for simple purposes,

22
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.260
and the panel configuration

23
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.760
with a 2.8-millimeter pixel pitch

24
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.859
is pretty good for a small environment,

25
00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:24.659
though an even tighter pixel pitch

26
00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.590
would probably be a little bit better.

27
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.730
All right, for this particular stage,

28
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.909
I'm showing a simple wired diagram

29
00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:36.189
of how the signal flow is.

30
00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:39.950
You have house time code and house genlock,

31
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.370
which are feeding specific signals to the mocap network,

32
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.730
the camera that's filming the LED wall,

33
00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.170
any type of recording or capture material,

34
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.950
like the Q-Take at the LA R&D stage,

35
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.489
or our simple little Kona field recorder

36
00:01:57.489 --> 00:01:59.250
and Kona capture card

37
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.370
in our facility here in our control machine.

38
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:03.909
Time code and genlock signals

39
00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.010
are also sent to each control computer

40
00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.889
and also to each render computer.

41
00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:14.810
The render computers are equipped with the A6000 GPUs.

42
00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.370
They need to be genlocked

43
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.090
to make sure that they are drawing pixels

44
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.990
in synchronization to the actual wall.

45
00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.370
The video signals from the render computer,

46
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.689
along with the time code and genlock

47
00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.770
that's being transmitted,

48
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.849
will leave those computers

49
00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:36.009
and be routed over to the LED processors.

50
00:02:36.009 --> 00:02:39.250
And the LED processors in this particular facility,

51
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.870
the Latte Stage,

52
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.129
this is being driven by the Brompton Tesra SX40.

53
00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:48.449
So it receives a video signal in via HDMI

54
00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.310
in this particular case,

55
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:54.650
and it also receives time code in and genlock in.

56
00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.090
The Tesra will then take that video signal

57
00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.330
and it'll convert it

58
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.930
to a 10 gigabit fiber video over IP signal

59
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.169
and send it to the distribution units.

60
00:03:06.169 --> 00:03:08.650
So they're the Brompton distribution units.

61
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.849
They'll take that 10 gigabits video over IP signal

62
00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:16.849
and break it into smaller one gigabit over video IP signals

63
00:03:17.169 --> 00:03:20.370
and route that signal and daisy chain it

64
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.530
to each individual panel.

65
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.930
Now, the way that that is configured

66
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.039
is handled through the Tesra software.

67
00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:30.180
So if we look at that software,

68
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.759
Brompton will give you the way

69
00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:34.719
to specifically configure how the panels

70
00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:37.199
will draw and handle that signal.

71
00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:38.719
So if you look on the slide here,

72
00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:43.719
you see that we have A1, A2, A3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8.

73
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.280
These each have the specific signal flow

74
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.759
as to which panels are hooked up

75
00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:51.379
and handled by the processor.

76
00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:54.319
And if we look with my camera here,

77
00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:58.560
you can see that we have those individual regions

78
00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.599
on the screen.

79
00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:04.599
One will handle between panels E01 through A01,

80
00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.860
where two will handle E02 all the way up and over

81
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.370
and down, and then you have 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8.

82
00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:20.370
And each one of these are gonna be controlling

83
00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:23.649
the way the video signal is handled

84
00:04:23.649 --> 00:04:26.129
and processed by the wall.

85
00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:29.089
Now for cameras within side of this facility,

86
00:04:29.089 --> 00:04:31.230
there's an array of different choices.

87
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.370
We normally like to kind of categorize our high end camera

88
00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.850
versus our mid range camera and a low end camera.

89
00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.930
So for the NAND stage and the LA R&D stage,

90
00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:46.129
we're running with an Arri Alexa Mini LF camera.

91
00:04:46.129 --> 00:04:49.529
This is a large format sensor, full frame.

92
00:04:49.529 --> 00:04:54.529
It has no crop factor, essentially 0.98 or 1X crop factor.

93
00:04:54.649 --> 00:04:57.009
It is a rolling shutter sensor

94
00:04:57.009 --> 00:05:02.009
and the body price comes clocking in at around $60,000.

95
00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:05.209
So it is a fairly high end camera.

96
00:05:05.209 --> 00:05:08.329
Once you finally get it all decked out with lenses

97
00:05:08.329 --> 00:05:10.970
and video assist and your tripod

98
00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.050
and any other additional accessories you might want,

99
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.310
like a follow focus and so forth,

100
00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.569
this particular high end camera can hit six figures

101
00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:21.790
at times.

102
00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:25.009
The mid range that we're using here at the Latte stage,

103
00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:30.009
this is a Blackmagic Ursa Mini camera, 4.6K generation II.

104
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.529
It's a super 35 millimeter sensor, a little bit smaller,

105
00:05:34.689 --> 00:05:38.490
but it does have a crop factor of 1.5X

106
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.209
and it also is a rolling shutter based camera.

107
00:05:42.209 --> 00:05:45.290
Finally, for more independent or low entry level

108
00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:48.889
or broadcast solution cameras that you might wanna use

109
00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:51.649
for indie production or for broadcast production,

110
00:05:51.649 --> 00:05:53.850
we've got like the Panasonic BGH1,

111
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.750
which I have sitting here on my desk.

112
00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.250
This is a micro four thirds sensor.

113
00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:00.410
It has a crop factor of 2X

114
00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.970
and a body price of about two grand.

115
00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.009
This system fully decked out right around $5,000

116
00:06:07.009 --> 00:06:10.769
with your individual video monitor,

117
00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:12.449
lenses, the camera body,

118
00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:15.329
and any other extras that you might want.

119
00:06:15.329 --> 00:06:18.430
Now, when we talk about cameras,

120
00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.029
there's something that you should really be aware of.

121
00:06:21.029 --> 00:06:23.069
There are basically two output signals

122
00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:24.550
that you can have from a camera,

123
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.060
whether it's SDI or HDMI.

124
00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.819
There is a general preference

125
00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:32.379
that you'll find at most studios.

126
00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:35.300
Most studios will rather work with SDI,

127
00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.819
serial digital interface, versus HDMI,

128
00:06:38.819 --> 00:06:42.939
which is high definition multimedia interface.

129
00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:46.579
Now, SDI has a big advantage

130
00:06:46.579 --> 00:06:50.220
in the sense that it can run longer cable lengths

131
00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.899
than HDMI.

132
00:06:51.899 --> 00:06:53.060
After a certain point,

133
00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.899
HDMIs might require repeaters

134
00:06:55.899 --> 00:06:59.860
in order to potentially run a longer cable.

135
00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.779
And anytime that you're adding in additional repeaters

136
00:07:03.779 --> 00:07:05.420
or signal enhancers,

137
00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:08.579
there's a chance that you might introduce some latency.

138
00:07:08.579 --> 00:07:12.060
But SDI comes in a number of different flavors as well.

139
00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:16.800
There's HD SDI, which is a 720p signal

140
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.339
running at 1.48 gigabits per second

141
00:07:19.339 --> 00:07:21.259
worth of data and information,

142
00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:23.779
all the way up to 12G SDI,

143
00:07:23.779 --> 00:07:28.079
which will handle a 2160p 60 frames per second signal.

144
00:07:28.079 --> 00:07:31.639
And it's running at 12 gigabits per second

145
00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:33.759
worth of data and information.

146
00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:36.839
So the thing to remember about this,

147
00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:41.839
the faster your SDI speed gets, like the 12G SDI,

148
00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:46.439
the more sensitive these particular cables

149
00:07:46.439 --> 00:07:49.800
and circuitry can be to voltage issues and problems.

150
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.319
So you're always wanting to remember

151
00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:53.600
to make sure that you're dealing

152
00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.279
with grounded components and equipment,

153
00:07:56.279 --> 00:07:57.800
that your power is hooked up

154
00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.279
before you start hooking up your BNC cables.

155
00:08:01.279 --> 00:08:02.899
If you don't have a grounded source

156
00:08:02.899 --> 00:08:05.079
and you plug in an SDI cable,

157
00:08:05.079 --> 00:08:07.040
there's a chance that you might short out

158
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.720
the SDI circuitry and completely destroy your camera.

159
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.040
And that's not something that you wanna do.

160
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.980
Talking about timecode.

161
00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.620
Timecode, in essence, it's a signal

162
00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:22.620
and a way to divide the measurement of time across frames.

163
00:08:23.139 --> 00:08:27.660
So timecode generators will synchronize the first frame

164
00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.680
across all common devices that are hooked up

165
00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.779
to a timecode generator.

166
00:08:32.779 --> 00:08:36.620
Normally you'll have one in your machine room

167
00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.179
along with a genlock generator as well.

168
00:08:40.179 --> 00:08:42.919
There are two flavors of timecode.

169
00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:45.659
You have linear timecode or LTC,

170
00:08:45.659 --> 00:08:49.940
which is basically a signal that will be written

171
00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:51.759
onto the audio track.

172
00:08:51.759 --> 00:08:55.860
Whereas there is a vertical interval timecode

173
00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.000
or VITC timecode that can actually be written

174
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.679
into the video track.

175
00:09:00.679 --> 00:09:04.080
So the big difference between the two,

176
00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.399
VITC timecode will allow you to scrub your playback

177
00:09:08.399 --> 00:09:10.960
or slow down or speed up your playback

178
00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.799
and it'll maintain timecode effectively.

179
00:09:13.799 --> 00:09:17.399
Whereas linear timecode doesn't have that option.

180
00:09:17.399 --> 00:09:21.039
Like I said, it's being written onto your audio track

181
00:09:21.159 --> 00:09:24.799
and you cannot deploy any kind of slowing down

182
00:09:24.799 --> 00:09:28.480
or speeding up of playback speed, recording speed,

183
00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:29.759
or scrubbing that at all.

184
00:09:29.759 --> 00:09:32.639
So there is a limitation with LTC.

185
00:09:32.639 --> 00:09:37.220
The big advantage of LTC is it's pretty much more common.

186
00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:41.320
And there's a number of ways that you can use LTC

187
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.200
and take that recorded material and synchronize it

188
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.600
in your software.

189
00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.240
Like whether you're using some form

190
00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.179
of nonlinear editing tools to put this all together.

191
00:09:53.179 --> 00:09:58.019
There is a process that we commonly call jamming timecode.

192
00:09:58.019 --> 00:10:02.860
Now on some cameras, the Blackmagic Ursa in particular,

193
00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:07.179
it only has one BNC port to actually handle

194
00:10:07.179 --> 00:10:09.980
either timecode or genlock.

195
00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.980
And I'm gonna pick up my camera here.

196
00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.639
I'm gonna show you what that looks like

197
00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:15.580
on the Blackmagic.

198
00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.820
So if we look at the Blackmagic camera,

199
00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:22.419
you're gonna see that there are three ports here.

200
00:10:22.419 --> 00:10:24.879
I'm gonna move this out of the way so you can see.

201
00:10:24.879 --> 00:10:27.320
You can see that we have an SDI out,

202
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.139
we have an SDI in,

203
00:10:29.139 --> 00:10:32.100
and we have reference in and timecode in.

204
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.179
It's this bottom port down here

205
00:10:34.179 --> 00:10:36.740
that handles both timecode and genlock

206
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.179
for this particular camera.

207
00:10:39.179 --> 00:10:42.399
There is one more SDI port up here in the front,

208
00:10:42.399 --> 00:10:45.799
but this basically will lead to your field monitor

209
00:10:45.799 --> 00:10:46.940
that we have attached here.

210
00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.080
This is the Atomos Shogun.

211
00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:54.080
But getting back to jamming timecode,

212
00:10:54.279 --> 00:10:56.779
the nice thing about most professional cameras

213
00:10:56.779 --> 00:11:00.759
is that they have their own timecode clock inside of it.

214
00:11:00.759 --> 00:11:03.200
And that clock is very accurate.

215
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.360
And when you jam timecode,

216
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.360
you're basically taking the timecode cable,

217
00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.100
in this case, it's this one.

218
00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:12.820
Where is it?

219
00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.320
There, timecode in.

220
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.340
You would plug this into this port

221
00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.860
and it would synchronize your camera's clock

222
00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:24.259
to the house clock in your machine room.

223
00:11:24.259 --> 00:11:26.539
Once that process takes place,

224
00:11:26.539 --> 00:11:30.419
you can unplug timecode and plug in genlock

225
00:11:30.419 --> 00:11:32.700
and it will then genlock the camera

226
00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.419
to your genlock generator in your machine room.

227
00:11:35.419 --> 00:11:37.620
So the only time that you need

228
00:11:37.659 --> 00:11:39.659
to continue jamming timecode

229
00:11:39.659 --> 00:11:42.740
is probably a couple times a day

230
00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.899
because there is the possibility

231
00:11:44.899 --> 00:11:47.960
for your camera's clock to drift over time.

232
00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.519
And a simple jamming will take care of that.

233
00:11:51.519 --> 00:11:53.940
So I would recommend that you jam timecode

234
00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.899
when you first come in during the day,

235
00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:00.450
probably over lunch and then later in the afternoon.

236
00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.870
I mentioned genlock.

237
00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.230
Well, what is genlock?

238
00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:06.710
Genlock is another type of syncing signal

239
00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:09.909
that helps with making sure that everything

240
00:12:09.909 --> 00:12:14.210
is drawing your pixels and scan line information

241
00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.629
at the same time.

242
00:12:15.629 --> 00:12:20.820
So genlock, your machine room will likely have

243
00:12:20.899 --> 00:12:25.899
a genlock generator rack-mounted in your systems there.

244
00:12:26.279 --> 00:12:30.679
That genlock will then be transmitted via wires

245
00:12:30.679 --> 00:12:32.820
to all of your various devices

246
00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.059
within your ICVFX facility.

247
00:12:35.059 --> 00:12:36.820
It will go to your control machines,

248
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:40.419
your render machines, your cameras, your field recorders,

249
00:12:40.419 --> 00:12:45.419
anything that relies on making sure things are synchronized.

250
00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:50.860
So in your render machines in particular,

251
00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:54.299
this is extremely essential

252
00:12:54.299 --> 00:12:56.779
because when you have an LED volume,

253
00:12:56.779 --> 00:12:59.200
especially a large LED volume,

254
00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.580
that volume is probably being divided up

255
00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.539
amongst a number of different nodes

256
00:13:03.539 --> 00:13:06.940
that are handling specific portions of the screen.

257
00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.960
Those nodes, which are handled by end display,

258
00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:14.940
are all being rendered on different computers.

259
00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.340
And each computer needs to make sure

260
00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:20.539
that they are rendering the graphics together

261
00:13:20.539 --> 00:13:22.340
at the same time.

262
00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.179
If you don't have that happen,

263
00:13:24.179 --> 00:13:26.019
as you see in this particular example

264
